# ── Chapter 28 fidelity pins: "When the System Notices It Broke" ─────────────
#
# The justice-specific continuation is also exercised by:
#   tests/pins/justice/defect/downstream-withdrawal-and-fresh-correction/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/justice/defect/wrong-version-cannot-withdraw-real-relief/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/justice/nonresponse/positive/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/justice/nonresponse/silence-without-positive-elapsed-evidence/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/justice/appeal/request-without-operator-or-previous-permission/expect.pins.nibli
#
# "Withdrawing a defective authority" rests on these actual-constitution
# withdrawal sequences:
#   tests/pins/knowledge/free-field/restriction-defect-and-rights-sequence/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/knowledge/free-field/unrelated-defect/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/record-power/power/holding-defect-and-rights-sequence/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/record-power/power/unrelated-defect/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/scarcity/priority/false-scarcity-stops-the-allocation/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/scarcity/priority/not-a-shortage-BudgetChoice/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/knowledge/free-field/restriction-does-not-travel-between-fields/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/record-power/power/holding-does-not-cover-another-subject/expect.pins.nibli
# Each asserts a reviewed defect and withdraws the affected permission while
# protective duties and the person's standing, floor and ballot remain. These
# sequences do not establish that any correction was made or published.
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/28-when-the-system-notices-it-broke.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # all substantive cases and contradiction checks
#
# The two-place Review and Appeals duties feed the exact typed three-place
# duty, and `obliged` is a derived conclusion rather than a writable verdict.
# The two source checks below hold each of those lines to exactly one
# occurrence, because a duplicate consumer or declaration is also a defect.
#
# Positive custody-condition findings have their own case, place, period and
# independent witnesses. Missing company evidence is not an isolation finding.
# Routine custody review and complaint access do not wait for such a finding.
# The complete condition and explicit weaker-rule cases are in:
#   tests/pins/custody/condition-findings/expect.pins.nibli
#   tests/pins/custody/condition-findings/counterfactual.pins.nibli

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closes the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact aborts the run rather than loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

# The ecological and animal continuation belongs to Chapter 6, "Who Owes, and
# What Follows", and its probe is in that chapter's pin file.
:expect-pins 37

# The source-bound finding/action contract reads the two-place duty only after
# the supplied initial-finding standard and its other typed evidence rejoin
# it. The result is a formal duty, never proof that anybody acted.
:require test "$(grep -F -c '& obliged($reader, $subject) -> obliged($reader, $reader_duty, $reader_standard).' book-1/source/constitution.nibli)" -eq 1
:require test "$(grep -F -c 'derived_only("obliged").' book-1/source/constitution.nibli)" -eq 1

# "The record holds company receipts for none of the prisoners in it, and from
# that gap the rules conclude isolation for nobody."
? err(Adam, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Hano, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Lalo, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Nando, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Faro, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Kel, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Ruk, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# Relief and protected disclosures are not condition reports either.
? err(Nia, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Iris, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Lior, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# Bela and Jala are free, and an isolation finding needs "a physically held
# person", so none follows for either.
? err(Bela, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Jala, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# The shipped record supplies no matching company receipt for either case.
? meets(Hano).
# => FALSE

? meets(Bela).
# => FALSE

# "The placement marker reads a reported act" and compares it with the
# authorised destination. "With no placement reported, the marker is silent
# about Ruk, about another person held in secure custody and about Hano."
# Chapter 26's pins show it firing on a reported misplacement.
? err(Ruk, Placement).
# => FALSE

? err(Lalo, Placement).
# => FALSE

? err(Hano, Placement).
# => FALSE

# The authority in force for Ruk names a secure facility; with no report set
# against it, the marker has nothing to compare.
? building(HighSec, Ruk).
# => TRUE

# Routine custody review is owed without an isolation or placement finding.
? obliged(Review, Ruk).
# => TRUE

? obliged(Review, Lalo).
# => TRUE

# The automatic review duty also reaches these custodial cases.
? obliged(Review, Adam).
# => TRUE

? obliged(Review, Hano).
# => TRUE

# THE ARGUMENT-ORDER DISCRIMINATOR — DO NOT DROP EITHER HALF. Writing the duty
# backwards leaves the four pins above TRUE while silently meaning the PRISONER is
# bound to the Review. These two are the only thing that tells the versions apart.
# The converse spelling `obligated_by`, used in a rule head, concludes
# `obliged` with its two arguments swapped, and a slip in argument order
# within `obliged` itself is caught by nothing but these two lines.
? obliged(Ruk, Review).
# => FALSE

? obliged(Adam, Review).
# => FALSE

# silent where the markers are silent: Bela is not confined, Dara is shielded.
? obliged(Review, Bela).
# => FALSE

? obliged(Review, Dara).
# => FALSE

# Dara is judged but not held, since the shield blocks confinement, so no
# isolation marker follows for Dara either.
? err(Dara, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# ── The simpler extension, accepted and left resident ───────────────────────
# "A simpler extension is tried here as well: mark a breach of duty whenever
# the review body owes a person review and no entry records its response.
# Added to the rules, it marks a breach for Ruk, which shows that further
# consequences can be written." This is that rule. It makes err and obliged
# mutually recursive over positive edges, and the stratifier permits it. The
# constitution's own route asks instead for "positive evidence of
# non-response, since a missing entry is only silence".
#
# The accept is unscoped because the rule is the premise of the query below
# it: `:accept-scoped` would retract the rule before the query, which would
# then answer FALSE, keeping the half of the claim that the rule loads and
# losing the half that it derives. An accepted rule stays in the KB, so every
# pin below runs with it; it concludes nothing for the child, to whom the
# review body owes no duty.
:accept
all $x: obliged(Review, $x) & ~capture(Review, $x) -> err($x, Duty).

? err(Ruk, Duty).
# => TRUE

# ── The child with nobody ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The birth-only fixture supplies no positive condition finding or placement
# report. No custodial review duty follows from the birth entry alone.
? err(Nell, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Nell, Placement).
# => FALSE

# "The review body's duty follows confinement, a marker or a complaint about
# custody conditions, and the rules find none of them for Nell".
? prisoner(Nell).
# => FALSE

? obliged(Review, Nell).
# => FALSE

# "Suppose a further rule were added: a marker that fires whenever a delivery
# is owed and unrecorded. It fires for Nell." That run is
# tests/pins/records/child_with_nobody/undelivered.pins.nibli, against the
# base counterfactual/undelivered-marker. Here, on the shipped source, the
# marker does not exist; the ordinary floor and assistance duties remain
# separate.
? owe(State, Eats, Nell).
# => TRUE

# The debt stands while no receipt shows food arriving: "Nell is owed food,
# and nothing in the record shows food received".
? eats(Nell).
# => FALSE
